# The one-off production import (AOC-040). Railway builds this as a THIRD service in the same
# project — beside the site and the backup job — with NO cronSchedule: it runs when asked.
#
# ⭐ WHY THIS EXISTS AT ALL, in one measurement. The production database has no public endpoint
# by design, so the only route in from a laptop is the SSH tunnel of docs/runbook-restore.md § 1.
# Through that tunnel one round trip costs 273 ms, and the importer writes ~49,800 rows one
# statement at a time: ≈ 3.8 hours in a single transaction riding on an SSH connection. The dry
# run died at item 418 of 4,648 on the binary's own 10-minute deadline (AOC-034, 2026-09-23).
# Raising the deadline buys a longer way to fail. Running beside the database removes the tunnel.
#
# ---- how the snapshot gets in, which is the whole design question -----------
#
# `items_clean.json` is 10 MB and lives in a SEPARATE, PRIVATE repo
# (github.com/pierrehrt/aoc-armory-snapshot). Railway builds from a repo that does not contain
# it. This image is therefore deployed with `railway up <context>` from a machine that holds
# both checkouts — the build context is assembled, not cloned. See docs/architecture.md § Deploy.
#
# Rejected, and why (AOC-040, DECISIONS.md 2026-09-23):
#   * fetch from R2 at runtime — the backups bucket EXPIRES objects after 30 days, so the
#     snapshot would silently vanish; and the tooltip bucket is served publicly at
#     img.aoc-codex.app, so putting the corpus there publishes it.
#   * clone the private repo at build time — a GitHub token inside a Railway build, which is a
#     credential in a third place. AOC-030's design went out of its way to avoid exactly that.
#   * commit the snapshot into this repo — 10 MB in the app repo and a SECOND copy of the corpus
#     free to drift from the canonical one. That is the "two descriptions of one fact" shape that
#     produced AOC-037 and all five AOC-012 defects.

# ---- build ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Pinned to match go.mod (go 1.23) and Dockerfile — bumping Go means bumping all three, in the
# same commit.
FROM golang:1.23-alpine AS build
WORKDIR /src

COPY go.mod go.sum ./
RUN go mod download

COPY . .

RUN CGO_ENABLED=0 GOOS=linux go build -trimpath -ldflags "-s -w" \
      -o /out/import-armory ./cmd/import-armory

# ---- the job ----------------------------------------------------------------
# alpine rather than the site's `scratch`, and the reason is the same one that makes
# Dockerfile.backup alpine: this is a JOB whose arguments change between runs. The dry run and
# the real run differ by one flag, and Railway's start command needs a shell to override it.
# Forcing a rebuild to change a flag is how a dry run gets skipped "just this once".
FROM alpine:3.22

# TLS roots. The importer talks to Postgres over the private network, but pgx negotiates TLS and
# a missing root fails at RUN time, not build time — the worst moment to find out.
RUN apk add --no-cache ca-certificates

COPY --from=build /out/import-armory /usr/local/bin/import-armory

# The corpus, from the assembled build context. ⚠️ Baked in on purpose: the image digest then
# pins exactly which snapshot produced a given import, which is what makes the run reproducible
# (AOC-034 — "everything the import writes is reproducible from armory_snapshot/").
COPY armory_snapshot/items_clean.json /snapshot/items_clean.json

# Connects out and writes nothing to disk; it needs nothing root provides.
USER 65532:65532

# ⛔ THE DEFAULT IS A DRY RUN, deliberately. Committing to production requires overriding this
# start command in Railway, which is a visible, separate act rather than a redeploy that happens
# to write. `-confirm-host` is the importer's own guard and is not optional on a non-local host.
#
# A job MUST exit. Railway's restartPolicyType is NEVER on this service for the same reason the
# backup job has it: a job that lingers is a job nobody can tell has finished.
ENTRYPOINT ["/usr/local/bin/import-armory"]
CMD ["-dry-run", "-snapshot", "/snapshot/items_clean.json", "-confirm-host", "postgres.railway.internal:5432"]
